gives you the ability
to do more with less.

00:15:07.730 --> 00:15:11.030
As an example, Google
Cloud uses our intelligence

00:15:11.030 --> 00:15:14.670
to automate the cooling
of our own data centers.

00:15:14.670 --> 00:15:17.520
Top of the mind
for every customer

00:15:17.520 --> 00:15:22.380
is the total amount of spend
that they have for their cloud.

00:15:22.380 --> 00:15:25.800
Visibility, control,
and optimized cost

00:15:25.800 --> 00:15:27.660
are very important.

00:15:27.660 --> 00:15:32.130
Google Cloud offers coveted
use discounts or CUDs

00:15:32.130 --> 00:15:37.200
that provide the best possible
pricing through deep discounts

00:15:37.200 --> 00:15:39.560
against long term commitments.

00:15:39.560 --> 00:15:45.290
Recently, we expanded our CUDs
to allow for multi project

00:15:45.290 --> 00:15:48.800
coverage where you could go
ahead and then apply cuts

00:15:48.800 --> 00:15:51.350
through multiple
parallel projects

00:15:51.350 --> 00:15:54.240
that you have as part
of your organization.

00:15:54.240 --> 00:15:58.190
In addition to that, we have
a number of new capabilities

00:15:58.190 --> 00:16:02.420
that are coming up, including
auto-renewing your CUDs

00:16:02.420 --> 00:16:04.520
as they come up on their
one year or three year

00:16:04.520 --> 00:16:08.540
anniversaries, and also, the
ability to schedule the start

00:16:08.540 --> 00:16:11.870
and stop of your VMs
in order to right size

00:16:11.870 --> 00:16:14.420
them at any given
time to conserve

00:16:14.420 --> 00:16:16.760
the total use of the
number, of course,

00:16:16.760 --> 00:16:18.980
that you would have as
part of your commits.

00:16:18.980 --> 00:16:22.070
It also does suspend
and resume VMs as part

00:16:22.070 --> 00:16:25.460
of this overall offering.

00:16:25.460 --> 00:16:29.640
Patch management is an
incredibly hard problem

00:16:29.640 --> 00:16:33.540
to manage, and typically
presents a significant burden

00:16:33.540 --> 00:16:36.120
as part of infrastructure
management.

00:16:36.120 --> 00:16:38.700
Google Cloud lifts
that burden away

00:16:38.700 --> 00:16:43.110
by leveraging native OS
tools and automating the task

00:16:43.110 --> 00:16:45.830
of patch management for you.

00:16:45.830 --> 00:16:50.210
We have wide coverage
of all popular operating

00:16:50.210 --> 00:16:55.670
systems spanning from Windows
to several flavors of Linux.

00:16:55.670 --> 00:16:59.600
Google Cloud also provides a
number of different controls

00:16:59.600 --> 00:17:03.410
that allow you to target
specific hosts as part

00:17:03.410 --> 00:17:07.270
of your overall infrastructure
within Google cloud.

00:17:07.270 --> 00:17:09.160
And also provide
the ability for you

00:17:09.160 --> 00:17:12.130
to schedule your
maintenance windows

00:17:12.130 --> 00:17:15.099
and orchestrate
your patch actions

00:17:15.099 --> 00:17:20.480
as part of an overall
patching capability.

00:17:20.480 --> 00:17:23.819
Last but not least on
the operational end,

00:17:23.819 --> 00:17:27.680
we're also providing OS config
management capabilities that

00:17:27.680 --> 00:17:29.870
are today at beta.

00:17:29.870 --> 00:17:33.490
Managing configuration
and configuration drifts

00:17:33.490 --> 00:17:36.800
is a hard problem, and
we aim to make that easy.

00:17:36.800 --> 00:17:41.010
With this new beta, an
OS config management,

00:17:41.010 --> 00:17:43.370
we're presenting a
compliance dashboard

00:17:43.370 --> 00:17:48.330
that gives you details on
config across Linux and Windows

00:17:48.330 --> 00:17:50.490
and across hybrid clouds.

00:17:50.490 --> 00:17:53.030
You can use third
party config tools

00:17:53.030 --> 00:17:56.750
if you choose to bootstrap
packages and deploy

00:17:56.750 --> 00:18:00.620
and maintain configurations
coupled with drift prevention

00:18:00.620 --> 00:18:02.740
very easily.

00:18:02.740 --> 00:18:05.260
With that in mind,
if we kind of look

00:18:05.260 --> 00:18:09.130
